#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163160} :activity/id #uuid "fc8ae5eb-5ab5-597c-afc2-a92953484868" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051075 :topic/id #uuid "7ef16e52-fe9f-5d49-a45f-2293e652f044" :topic/math-academy-id 3036} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163369 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn about the **supremum** of a set.\n\nTo get started, we first need to understand a related concept: the **upper bounds** of a set.\n\nGiven a set $A$, a number $x$ is called an upper bound for $A$ if every element of $A$ is smaller than or equal to $x$.\nAlternatively, $x$ is greater than or equal to every element of $A$.\n\nFor example, consider\n\n$$\nA = \\{1,2,3\\}\\subseteq \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nIn this case, any number\n\n$$\nx\\geq 3\n$$\n\nis an upper bound of $A$, since it is greater than or equal to $1, 2$, and $3$.\nSo, $[3, \\infty)$ is the set of upper bounds of $A$.\n\nWe will denote the set of the upper bounds of $A$ as $U_A$.\n\nWe can now proceed with the definition of **supremum**.\n\nFor a set $A$ that is bounded above, the supremum of $A$ is the minimum of $U_A$. It is also called the **least upper bound** of $A$.\n\nIn our example\n\n$$\nA = \\{1,2,3\\}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\min(U_A) = \\min [3, \\infty) = 3\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\sup(A) = 3\n$$\n\nNotice that, when a set has a maximum, the supremum equals the maximum. This holds, in particular, for non-empty finite sets.\n\nThis leads to the following result:\n\n> For any non-empty finite set, $\\max(A) = \\sup(A)$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca59d62a-e7c7-5a90-b351-f1caf0f39ac0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9405 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c153f719-0723-5000-ab9b-87cfd2f9bbb3" :step/math-academy-id 629493 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163161}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163203 :tutorial/content "Just as we defined the supremum, we now introduce the **infimum** of a set.\n\nAs we did for the supremum, we first need to define the lower bounds of a set.\n\nGiven a set $A$, a number $x$ is called a **lower bound** for $A$ if every element of $A$ is greater than or equal to $x$, or, alternatively, if $x$ is less than or equal to every element of $A$.\n\nFor example, for\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3\\}\\subseteq \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nany number\n\n$$\nx \\leq 1\n$$\n\nis less than $1$, $2$, and $3$. Therefore, $(-\\infty, 1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A$.\n\nWe will denote the set of the lower bounds of $A$ as $L_A$.\n\nThe infimum of a set $A$ is the maximum of $L_A$, or the greatest lower bound of $A$.\n\nFor our set\n\n$$\nA = \\{1,2,3\\}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\max(L_A) = \\max (-\\infty, 1] = 1\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\inf(A) = 1\n$$\n\nAgain, we notice that the minimum of a set (provided that it exists) equals the infimum. As finite sets always have a minimum, this holds in particular for finite sets:\n\n> For any finite set, $\\min(A) = \\inf(A)$" :tutorial/id #uuid "51b90880-5036-599d-96af-8a4c20add3ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10844 :tutorial/title "Infimum"} :step/id #uuid "783f0d37-8fa2-5e94-a643-c928a45a5266" :step/math-academy-id 629494 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163162}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163162 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162156 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161741 :question/id #uuid "80eb38f0-092a-54fc-873c-6ab7b6be5307" :question/math-academy-id "e-16535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f950f3da-81c2-574a-b7f7-06d88945c249" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049900}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162529}] :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Finite Set"} :step/id #uuid "49b1de3d-7a36-5ac7-a8b3-9f98c01634c2" :step/math-academy-id 629495 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163163}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163163 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163200 :tutorial/content "Let's explore the supremum and infimum of intervals, starting with a closed interval.\n\n**Example A:** $A = [0, 1]$.\n\n**Observation (Min/Max):**\nRecall that if a set has a minimum and maximum, they correspond to the infimum and supremum.\nSince $0$ and $1$ are bounds contained in $A$, we have $\\min(A)=0$ and $\\max(A)=1$. Thus, we expect:\n\n$$\n\\inf (A) = 0, \\qquad \\sup (A) = 1\n$$\n\n**Derivation using Sets of Bounds:**\nTo deepen our understanding, we verify this by finding the sets of lower and upper bounds ($L_A$ and $U_A$).\n\n1. **Infimum:** We identify $L_A$ and find its maximum.Any number $x \\leq 0$ is a lower bound for $A$.Any number $x > 0$ is *not* a lower bound. Since $0 \\in A$ and $0 < x$, the number $x$ fails to be a lower bound.Therefore, $L_A = (-\\infty, 0]$ and $\\inf (A) = \\max (-\\infty, 0] = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}$.\n2. **Supremum:** We identify $U_A$ and find its minimum.Any number $x \\geq 1$ is an upper bound for $A$.Any number $x < 1$ is *not* an upper bound. Since $1 \\in A$ and $1 > x$, the number $x$ fails to be an upper bound.Therefore, $U_A = [1, \\infty)$ and $\\sup (A) = \\min [1, \\infty) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "46a24733-634e-584e-8d69-01df206cfe24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10841 :tutorial/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Closed Interval"} :step/id #uuid "c1a27dbd-9e07-56e1-9495-5799582ca8d5" :step/math-academy-id 629498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163164}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163164 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163210 :tutorial/content "We now consider intervals where the endpoints are not necessarily included.\n\n**Example B:** $B = (0, 1)$.\nThe endpoints $0$ and $1$ are not in $B$, so the set has **neither a maximum nor a minimum.**\n\nHowever, the logical boundaries of the set remain the same.\n\n- The set of lower bounds is $L_B = (-\\infty, 0]$.\n$\\inf (B) = \\max (-\\infty, 0] = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}$.\n- The set of upper bounds is $U_B = [1, \\infty)$.\n$\\sup (B) = \\min [1, \\infty) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "63a74372-be0b-55ee-88cf-ba10236de365" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14945 :tutorial/title "Supremum and Infimum of an Open Interval"} :step/id #uuid "14b7a7db-76cd-51a1-9831-3d3d23c70db6" :step/math-academy-id 629499 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163165}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163165 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163211 :tutorial/content "We continue by considering a half-open interval.\n\n**Example C:** $C = [0, 1)$.\nHere, the inclusion is mixed.\n\n- **Lower Bound:** $L_C = (-\\infty, 0]$.\n$\\inf (C) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}$\nSince $0 \\in C$, it is also the **minimum.**\n- **Upper Bound:** $U_C = [1, \\infty)$.\n$\\sup (C) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1}$\nSince $1 \\notin C$, the set has **no maximum.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "e886348b-6c7d-5c56-9ba6-8f18d48237e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14953 :tutorial/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Half-Open Interval"} :step/id #uuid "1be05391-d776-53e2-94a9-17169863be6e" :step/math-academy-id 629500 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163166}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163166 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163209 :tutorial/content "So far, we have dealt with bounded sets: sets that have both upper and lower bounds. Now, we consider unbounded sets.\n\nIf a set $A$ is unbounded above, then it has no upper bounds. In this case, we write\n\n$$\n\\sup(A) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if a set $A$ is unbounded below, then it has no lower bounds. In this case, we write\n\n$$\n\\inf(A) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the set\n\n$$\nA = (1, \\infty)\n$$\n\nThis set is unbounded above, so\n\n$$\n\\sup(A) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, $A$ is bounded below, and\n\n$$\n\\inf(A) = 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "205608a0-b120-56a1-b2c9-f33f2540e0b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14797 :tutorial/title "Unbounded Sets"} :step/id #uuid "340b9ca0-497a-5086-82a9-e785a2e094e9" :step/math-academy-id 629501 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163167}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162157 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161742 :question/id #uuid "a6b8b164-5249-58e0-a701-b6e6151c1881" :question/math-academy-id "e-16536"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee0e08c-5a7a-5f78-83aa-39679bb3b17b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050301}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162552}] :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "99437115-10cd-5290-aee8-0bbc4fa42ca2" :step/math-academy-id 629502 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163168}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163201 :tutorial/content "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\left(\\dfrac 12\\right)^n \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence $a_n$, given by\n\n$$\na_n =\\left(\\dfrac 12\\right)^n, \\qquad n \\in \\mathbb N = \\{1,2,3,4,\\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/bb/bb46c2cd565e33b9da6e6ef1496a0726212ebc18a425b915d3ef0d2acacef058.png)\n\nNotice that $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence. Therefore, the first term $a_1$ is the maximum of the set. Since $A$ has a maximum, the supremum equals the maximum:\n\n$$\n\\sup(A) = \\max(A) = a_1 = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nNow, our set doesn't have a minimum: the points of the sequence get closer and closer to $0$ without reaching it. Let's find the infimum by determining the set of the lower bounds.\n\nNotice that:\n\n- any number $x \\leq 0$ is a lower bound of $A$,\n- any number $x > 0$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$. In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $\\left(\\dfrac 12\\right)^n$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x > 0$.\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty, 0]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A$, and\n\n$$\n\\inf(A) = \\max \\big({-\\infty}, 0\\big] = 0\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "90180d58-a7d9-562b-91a3-cccac21bfc91" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10842 :tutorial/title "Sequences"} :step/id #uuid "1a3ee896-b5b1-59d9-a79e-d3371f921784" :step/math-academy-id 629506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163169}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162158 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161743 :question/id #uuid "ed21dea5-5c8b-5193-8fe9-4f1105410843" :question/math-academy-id "e-16537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe2b2c0-23a0-5bd0-8bc7-c12139820b61" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050155}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162530}] :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Sequence"} :step/id #uuid "a141f9b7-44be-5ea4-b45f-327b33819ac6" :step/math-academy-id 629507 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163170}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163202 :tutorial/content "The concepts of supremum and infimum can be extended to functions.\n\nGiven a function $y=f(x)$, the **supremum** and the **infimum** of $f$ are, respectively, the supremum and the infimum of the range of $f$.\n\nThe **supremum** and **infimum** of a function will be denoted as\n$\\inf_{x\\in D} f(x), \\qquad \\text{and} \\sup_{x\\in D} f(x)$,\nwhere $D$ is the domain of the function.\n\nFor example, consider the following parabola:\n\n![](images/69/690b179ad127181eeb316fb9ae9c8085fa6aa96c6d4729c88d176a908950e80e.png)\n\nThe range of $f$ is\n\n$$\nA = (-\\infty, 0]\n$$\n\nLet's find the infimum and the supremum of $A$.\n\nTo find the infimum, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no lower bounds. For any real number $y$, we can find an element $x$ such that $f(x) < y$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\inf_{x\\in \\mathbb R} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nNext, to find the supremum, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y \\geq 0$ is an upper bound of $A$,\n- any number $y < 0$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $0$, an element of $A$, is greater than $y$.\n\nTherefore, $[0,\\infty)$ is the set of upper bounds of $A$, and\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\sup_{x\\in \\mathbb R} f(x) = \\sup(A) =\\min[0,\\infty)= 0.}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "73cabff4-8bcc-5e0c-a0c6-0c53136c1ba0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10843 :tutorial/title "Functions"} :step/id #uuid "adb9b1a3-d36f-56f5-aed4-25013ca7485d" :step/math-academy-id 629510 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162159 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161744 :question/id #uuid "1a3afebe-554b-51c5-833c-431bbd669e91" :question/math-academy-id "e-16538"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4649783b-3436-55a5-b779-1ca3de17c535" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162532}] :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "ce6cca22-43d2-5f35-a1c3-e7ab58514145" :step/math-academy-id 629511 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163172}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163172 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163370 :tutorial/content "**Theorem.** Suppose $A\\subseteq \\mathbb R$ is a non-empty set and $s$ is an upper bound of $A$. Then $s = \\sup A$ if and only if for all $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $a\\in A$ such that\n$s - \\varepsilon < a$.\n\n- **First, we prove that if $s = \\sup A$ then for all $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $a\\in A$ such that $s - \\varepsilon < a$.**By contradiction, suppose that there exists some $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that there does not exist any $a\\in A$ with $s - \\varepsilon < a$. Then, every element of $A$ is less than or equal to $s - \\varepsilon$. This implies that $s - \\varepsilon$ is an upper bound of $A$.Since $s$ is the least upper bound, we must have $s \\le s - \\varepsilon$. However, since $\\varepsilon > 0$, we know that $s - \\varepsilon < s$. This is a contradiction.\n- **Next, we prove that if for all $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $a\\in A$ such that $s - \\varepsilon < a$, then $s = \\sup A$.**Again, by contradiction, assume $s \\neq \\sup A$. Since $s$ is an upper bound of $A$, the least upper bound must be strictly smaller than $s$. Let $s_1 = \\sup A$, so $s_1 < s$. Let $\\varepsilon_1 = s - s_1$. Since $s_1 < s$, we have $\\varepsilon_1 > 0$.\nWe can apply the property stated in the hypothesis to our $\\varepsilon_1$. So, there exists $a_1 \\in A$ such that\n$s - \\varepsilon_1 < a_1$.\nSubstituting $\\varepsilon_1 = s - s_1$, we get $s - (s - s_1) < a_1$, which simplifies to\n$s_1 < a_1$.\nSince $s_1$ is an upper bound of $A$, we must have $a_1 \\le s_1$. This contradicts $s_1 < a_1$. Thus, $s = \\sup A$." :tutorial/id #uuid "cced0110-6687-5d41-b7d0-e650638b956e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9407 :tutorial/title "An Important Lemma"} :step/id #uuid "34608cbd-8d66-523a-92fa-79ddb1f72cba" :step/math-academy-id 629514 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163173}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163204 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we defined the infimum of a set as the maximum of the set of lower bounds, and the supremum as the minimum of the set of upper bounds.\n\nIf a set of real numbers is bounded and not empty, then the infimum exists and is a real number, and the supremum exists and is a real number.\n\nThis follows from the so-called **axiom of completeness**, a fundamental property of the real numbers that ensures that the real number line has no \"gaps.\"\n\nThere are number sets, such as the set of rational numbers, that don't have this property. Consider for example the following subset of $\\mathbb Q{:}$\n\n$$\nA = \\{q \\in \\mathbb Q \\,: \\, 1 < q^2 < 2 \\}\n$$\n\nNotice that the rational numbers greater than $\\sqrt 2$ are the upper bounds of $A$. However, this set has no minimum *in $\\mathbb{Q}$*, so $\\sup(A)$ doesn't exist." :tutorial/id #uuid "22748d4c-0a29-59d0-ace0-00f9cea6dd28" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10853 :tutorial/title "The Completeness Axiom"} :step/id #uuid "8e86da46-eeeb-505c-861c-4485b44013f4" :step/math-academy-id 629515}] :activity/title "Supremum and Infimum" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162184}]}
